        if (JS_ToBigInt64(ctx, &v64, argv[2]))
            return JS_EXCEPTION;        ...```So Atomics.load doesn't call valueOf because it sets v=0 directly. Only the operations that write (add, sub, exchange, etc.) call valueOf.That means I can't avoid the write part. The issue is that writing arbitrary values corrupts heap metadata.What if I:
1. Read the current value first (leaking data)2. Write back the same value (no corruption)

But I can't do this in a single operation - the valueOf is called before the read!Wait, let me think about this more carefully. The sequence is:1. `js_atomics_get_ptr` captures stale pointer2. `JS_ToBigInt64(ctx, &v64, argv[2])` calls valueOf - buffer is resized here3. New allocation goes into freed space4. `atomic_exchange` is called with stale pointer - reads old value, writes new valueSo when step 4 executes, the stale pointer now points to the newly allocated buffer. 

When the atomic exchange runs, it reads whatever data is at that location in the new allocation, then overwrites it with the valueOf result. If that location contains tcache metadata, writing an invalid pointer corrupts the heap. I need to ensure the stale pointer lands on user-controlled data instead of metadata, or write a value that won't corrupt the tcache structure. One approach is to carefully arrange allocations so the target offset contains only user data, not pointers.
