Actually, looking at this more carefully, I realize the issue: `Atomics.exchange` both reads AND writes. The write part is what's causing corruption.

What if I use `Atomics.compareExchange`? It only writes if the current value matches the expected value. If I use an impossible expected value, it won't write!

```c
case ATOMICS_OP_COMPARE_EXCHANGE | (3 << 3):
    {
        uint64_t v1 = v;
        atomic_compare_exchange_strong((_Atomic(uint64_t) *)ptr, &v1, rep_val);
        a = v1;  // Returns the actual value
    }
```

So if I call `Atomics.compareExchange(view, idx, expectedValue, newValue)`:
1. It reads the current value at ptr
2. If current == expectedValue, it writes newValue
3. It returns the current value

If I use an expected value that will never match, it just reads without writing!

